WEBVTT


00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.844
Tanda and the Night the Network Woke.

00:00:03.844 --> 00:00:08.504
Elias had negotiated three more bites of corn.

00:00:08.504 --> 00:00:12.972
“Three kernels,” Dad said. “Rocket fuel.”

00:00:12.972 --> 00:00:17.776
He bit down. Something clicked against his spoon.

00:00:17.776 --> 00:00:21.212
“I meant to do that.”

00:00:21.212 --> 00:00:26.088
His tooth sat on the table, looking extremely pleased.

00:00:26.088 --> 00:00:29.764
Dad placed it in a little blue dish.

00:00:29.764 --> 00:00:32.456
“Want help writing the note?”

00:00:32.456 --> 00:00:38.316
“No. Then it would be your surprise.”

00:00:38.316 --> 00:00:42.088
Elias drew the corn as a rocket.

00:00:42.088 --> 00:00:48.260
Three kernels became windows. One orange line became flame.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:56.880
“I was brave. Also surprised. Keep the rocket part.”

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.804
He tucked tooth, note, and drawing beneath his pillow.

00:01:01.804 --> 00:01:06.536
Dad left the door open exactly one hand wide.

00:01:06.536 --> 00:01:12.132
Above the roofs, tiny Tanda followed a crowded golden route.

00:01:12.132 --> 00:01:17.728
She knew every latch, barking dog, and pretend sleeper.

00:01:17.728 --> 00:01:25.460
Lately, the route had doubled: more children, more windows, more fairies to train.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:35.112
“Were the mouse Keepers and roof birds falling behind, too? What about the tree guardians and great animal Keepers?”

00:01:35.112 --> 00:01:41.284
Elias's tooth felt warm with dinner, laughter, and surprise.

00:01:41.284 --> 00:01:48.872
Beyond Elias's window, another light flashed. Then another. Dawn was coming.

00:01:48.872 --> 00:01:52.308
“The picture can wait.”

00:01:52.308 --> 00:01:59.536
Tanda flew toward the next window. The folded drawing slipped onto the floor behind her.

00:01:59.536 --> 00:02:03.548
Its orange rocket flame faded to yellow.

00:02:03.548 --> 00:02:07.968
Two tiny eyes opened inside the nearest shadow.

00:02:07.968 --> 00:02:12.220
“Unread things are unattended things.”

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:16.760
It nibbled the flame, then one crooked window.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:21.324
“Please don't forget the rocket part.”

00:02:21.324 --> 00:02:25.072
Tanda stopped with one foot on the latch.

00:02:25.072 --> 00:02:30.308
The tooth remembered a child, a dinner, and a drawing.

00:02:30.308 --> 00:02:33.864
“But why did the corn matter?”

00:02:33.864 --> 00:02:40.588
“Once every story sounds the same, nobody notices what I take.”

00:02:40.588 --> 00:02:46.352
Names, jokes, and crooked little details were its favorite meal.

00:02:46.352 --> 00:02:51.060
Tanda opened the note, and the memory opened with it.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:58.864
Dad built a ridiculous rocket from three unwanted bites. Elias laughed—and tried.

00:02:58.864 --> 00:03:06.740
“Corn rocket. Three windows. A crooked orange flame.”

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:11.064
Each exact word returned a piece of the story.

00:03:11.064 --> 00:03:16.252
“Too much corn. And entirely too much listening.”

00:03:16.252 --> 00:03:22.400
Tooth, drawing, words, and laughter gathered into one memory.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:30.276
For the first time, Tanda saw the whole child inside the memory, held safely together.

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:35.608
Tanda opened her satchel and saw dozens of waiting notes.

00:03:35.608 --> 00:03:41.756
She opened the first one. A joke was already fading at the edges.

00:03:41.756 --> 00:03:49.680
“I kept every tooth,” she whispered. “But I did not keep every story safely.”

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:57.628
A small story could become more precious as a child grew—and impossible to replace once lost.

00:03:57.628 --> 00:04:02.288
Tanda had trained more fairies to reach more windows.

00:04:02.288 --> 00:04:08.388
But every route ended at a different desk, with a different piece of the story.

00:04:08.388 --> 00:04:17.344
A fairy could reach a window. A mouse could hear beneath a floor. A bird could carry a story across a roof.

00:04:17.344 --> 00:04:24.764
No Keeper working alone could keep every piece whole, permanent, and ready for the future.

00:04:24.764 --> 00:04:30.048
“Then we need a Network—one that remembers together.”

00:04:30.048 --> 00:04:38.764
Before dawn, Tanda wrote the same gold-edged invitation for every Keeper: We need to work together.

00:04:38.764 --> 00:04:46.568
In Madrid, the invitation waited inside Ratoncito Pérez's smallest mouse door.

00:04:46.568 --> 00:04:52.980
On a Korean roof, Kkachi found the same message tied beneath a tile.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:59.560
Under a Philippine eave, Daga discovered it beside three silver pawprints.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:06.572
Across a rainy Irish wall, Anna Bogle caught hers before the wind could steal it.

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:13.992
At the edge of the light, a great hyena Keeper found the call beside an ancient tree.

00:05:13.992 --> 00:05:20.548
Tanda's fairy colleagues carried the message along every golden route they knew.

00:05:20.548 --> 00:05:29.672
From roofs, roots, doorways, and gardens, different answers began to follow the same gold thread home.

00:05:29.672 --> 00:05:40.956
By morning, Elias's drawing was safe inside his first Toothlight—the corn rocket, the crooked flame, and the whole story together.

00:05:40.956 --> 00:05:47.056
“Good. The corn was the important part.”

00:05:47.056 --> 00:05:57.356
That was the night Tanda understood: stories needed a Network to stay whole, permanent, and able to carry value into the future.

00:05:57.356 --> 00:06:05.688
Across roofs, roots, doorways, and skies, the Keepers began building it together.
